// Sigil-Logic/clafer#31: a parenthesized negative integer literal as a
// reference target.  `neg -> (-1)` is unary minus over the literal in the
// IR (the grammar has no negative literals, and the unspaced `-> -1` is a
// syntax error); Common.getRefIds had no case for it, so every output mode
// crashed before any backend ran.  The literal now folds to -1 wherever a
// reference target is consumed: Alloy declares `one -1` (bare -- 6.2.0
// accepts it in declaration position and binds it tighter than the set
// operators), Choco emits `constant(-1)` on top of the #18 encoding, and
// facts keep the general rendering of unary minus.  The assertions hold
// only if the literal survives the fold with its sign, so the gated Choco
// leg guards the encoding end to end; the emission itself is baselined in
// the .als.reg and .js.reg.

neg -> (-1)
assert [ neg = -1 ]

// inside a set expression: the literal folds in place, then the set
// operators render around it
negOrOne -> (-1) ++ 1
assert [ negOrOne = -1 || negOrOne = 1 ]
